    "q-343760": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343760",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.",
      "worked_solution": "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
